<item>
            <title>[LU-20818] hybrid IO: single thread bandwidth limited by client-side serialization</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20818</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;Hybrid IO turns a large buffered IO into an unaligned direct IO, so&lt;br/&gt;
a single thread&apos;s bandwidth is set by how fast the client can build&lt;br/&gt;
RPCs and copy the user buffer.  Both are currently confined to one&lt;br/&gt;
CPU, and the threshold deciding when to switch predates large folio&lt;br/&gt;
support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Measured with one IOR thread on a 192 core client against 16&lt;br/&gt;
targets, with the targets faked (fail_loc 0x238) so the numbers are&lt;br/&gt;
the client path alone:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;1. RPC building runs on the submitting CPU.  Since 776e163cf542&lt;br/&gt;
   (&quot;&lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-17180&quot; title=&quot;don&apos;t block an ptlrpc for long time.&quot; class=&quot;issue-link&quot; data-issue-key=&quot;LU-17180&quot;&gt;&lt;del&gt;LU-17180&lt;/del&gt;&lt;/a&gt; ptlrpc: don&apos;t block ptlrpcd too long&quot;) each OSC&apos;s&lt;br/&gt;
   writeback work is queued on the system workqueue, which is&lt;br/&gt;
   per-CPU, so one thread&apos;s IO to a striped file is built on one&lt;br/&gt;
   core &amp;#8211; including the user copy of an unaligned DIO write.  A&lt;br/&gt;
   256 MiB unaligned write ran at 18 GiB/s.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;2. Moving to an unbound workqueue is not sufficient.  Since Linux&lt;br/&gt;
   6.5 the default affinity scope is the last level cache, so every&lt;br/&gt;
   OSC still builds inside the submitting CPU&apos;s CCD, 8 CPUs on this&lt;br/&gt;
   part.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;3. The unaligned copy is itself serial: one thread copies the whole&lt;br/&gt;
   IO into or out of the bounce buffer before the RPCs go out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With RPC building moved to a dedicated unbound workqueue, spread&lt;br/&gt;
over the submitting node, and the copy parallelized, the same&lt;br/&gt;
256 MiB unaligned write reaches 206 GiB/s.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Separately, the buffered-to-direct thresholds (2 MiB write, 8 MiB&lt;br/&gt;
read) were chosen before large folio support.  With folios enabled&lt;br/&gt;
the buffered write path is substantially faster, so the size at&lt;br/&gt;
which direct IO wins moves out; buffered reads gain nothing from&lt;br/&gt;
folios in this series while hybrid reads gain a great deal, so the&lt;br/&gt;
read threshold moves the other way.  The default should follow&lt;br/&gt;
whether folio support is on rather than adding a tunable.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
